<%#
  012 — the contents of the signed-in menu: every destination, the identity and
  the way out, in the order they are read in.

  This partial is the single source of truth for what the menu offers. It is
  rendered into both of the containers in shared/_site_menu — the narrow
  disclosure and the wide bar — exactly one of which is ever displayed, so FR-011
  ("the same capabilities at every width") holds because both containers are
  filled from here rather than because two lists were kept in agreement.
%>

<%# 003 FR-005: the "I'm looking for a locker" button lives on that page,
    so it has to be reachable from wherever the user happens to be. %>
<%= link_to t(".locker_wishes"), locker_wishes_path, class: "site-nav-link",
      "aria-current": ("page" if current_page?(locker_wishes_path)) %>

<%# 004 FR-015: the history is a separate screen, so it needs a way in
    from anywhere too. %>
<%= link_to t(".proposal_history"), locker_swap_proposals_path, class: "site-nav-link",
      "aria-current": ("page" if current_page?(locker_swap_proposals_path)) %>

<%# 013 FR-003/FR-004/FR-005: the administrator's menu, and nobody else's. The
    condition is what enforces FR-004 in the markup — a non-administrator's page
    is not sent this and then told to hide it, it is never sent it at all.

    That is presentation only. The refusal that matters is in the controller
    (ApplicationController#require_admin!), because a link left out of a page is
    no obstacle to an address typed into a bar.

    A nested <details> for the same reasons the menu around it is one (012): the
    open/closed state, the keyboard, and the announcement of both are native, so
    the submenu works with no JavaScript at all. Its contents are deliberately
    inside a closed disclosure — unlike the bar in 012, "not displayed until
    opened" is the behaviour being asked for here, not an accident to design
    around. %>
<% if current_user.admin? %>
  <details class="site-submenu">
    <summary class="site-submenu-toggle"><%= t(".admin") %></summary>

    <div class="site-submenu-panel">
      <%= link_to t(".users"), admin_users_path, class: "site-nav-link",
            "aria-current": ("page" if current_page?(admin_users_path)) %>
      <%# 031 FR-001: ordinary admin content, like "Users" above it — not the
          super-admin-only tier "Danger Zone" below narrows to (research.md
          R7). %>
      <%= link_to t(".locker_map"), admin_locker_map_path, class: "site-nav-link",
            "aria-current": ("page" if current_page?(admin_locker_map_path)) %>
      <%# 016 FR-001/FR-002: the second administrator destination. As above, that
          is presentation only: the refusal that matters is the guard on the
          controllers behind it.
          029 FR-007: this link narrows further than the submenu around it — the
          submenu (and "Users" above) stays open to every administrator, but this
          one link is now the super admin's alone, matching require_super_admin!
          on Admin::DangerZoneController/Admin::AllowedEmailDomainsController
          (research.md R4). %>
      <% if current_user.super_admin? %>
        <%= link_to t(".danger_zone"), admin_danger_zone_path, class: "site-nav-link",
              "aria-current": ("page" if current_page?(admin_danger_zone_path)) %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </details>
<% end %>

<%# 008: the email is what truncates on a narrow screen — never the
    brand, never the controls. %>
<span class="site-nav-identity"><%= current_user.email %></span>

<%= button_to t(".log_out"), destroy_user_session_path, method: :delete,
      class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
